    id: "d2e0126572f54d03d24f",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "After Israel’s election: What’s next?",
    summary: "Knesset member Ofer Cassif argues that the ‘politics of exclusion’ keep Israel in political deadlock.",
    preview: "The Bottom Line Knesset member Ofer Cassif argues that the ‘politics of exclusion’ keep Israel in political deadlock. The success of Palestinian-led parties – who won 15% of the vote in Israel’s elections – has created a conundrum for Israel’s leadership: partner with non-Zionists or accept political gridlock. While the bloc led by Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu got the most support from Israeli voters, it didn’t win enough seats in Israel’s parliament (Knesset) to build a ruling coalition so far.",
    body: "The Bottom Line\n\nKnesset member Ofer Cassif argues that the ‘politics of exclusion’ keep Israel in political deadlock.\n\nThe success of Palestinian-led parties – who won 15% of the vote in Israel’s elections – has created a conundrum for Israel’s leadership: partner with non-Zionists or accept political gridlock. While the bloc led by Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu got the most support from Israeli voters, it didn’t win enough seats in Israel’s parliament (Knesset) to build a ruling coalition so far.\n\nIsraeli politician Ofer Cassif, a member of one of the Palestinian-led parties, tells host Steve Clemons that the Israeli people “need to understand that the future must be a future of Jewish-Palestinian partnership and not another form of exclusion.”",
    content_status: "full",
