    title: "What’s the future of the Western Sahara conflict?",
    summary: "The UN is ending its peacekeeping mission in the disputed territory after the US and Russia blocked its extension.",
    preview: "Inside Story The United Nations ends its peacekeeping mission in the disputed territory. It’s one of Africa’s longest-running conflicts. The dispute over the territory of Western Sahara started more than 50 years ago. It began with the end of Spanish colonial rule in the region. Since then, both Morocco and the Polisario Front have claimed the Western Sahara. In the 1990s, the United Nations tried to reach a settlement, and deployed a peacekeeping mission to the region.",
    body: "Inside Story\n\nThe United Nations ends its peacekeeping mission in the disputed territory.\n\nIt’s one of Africa’s longest-running conflicts.\n\nThe dispute over the territory of Western Sahara started more than 50 years ago. It began with the end of Spanish colonial rule in the region.\n\nSince then, both Morocco and the Polisario Front have claimed the Western Sahara.\n\nIn the 1990s, the United Nations tried to reach a settlement, and deployed a peacekeeping mission to the region.\n\nLast week, that effort ended when Russia and the United States blocked proposals to extend the mission’s mandate.\n\nSo, could this development lead to renewed fighting?\n\nPresenter: Imran Khan\n\nGuests:\n\nSamir Bennis – Political analyst and author of the book The Self-Determination Delusion: How Activist Scholars and Journalists Have Hijacked the Western Sahara Case\n\nIrene Fernandez-Molina – Senior lecturer in International Relations at the University of Exeter in the United Kingdom and author of the book Moroccan Foreign Policy under Mohammed VI, 1999-2014\n\nMahjoub Mleiha – Advocacy head of Collective of Sahrawi Human Rights Defenders (CODESA)",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Al Jazeera",
    url: "https://www.aljazeera.com/video/inside-story/2026/11/2/whats-the-future-of-the-western-sahara-conflict?traffic_source=rss",
