    id: "2d3414095dd144db6ae8",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "China calls for Mideast dialogue in talks with Iran, Saudi, Oman",
    summary: "\"All parties should ... in a spirit of reason and pragmatism, focus on a political solution rather than once again resorting to armed conflict,\" said Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi.",
    preview: "\"All parties should ... in a spirit of reason and pragmatism, focus on a political solution rather than once again resorting to armed conflict,\" said Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi. BEIJING: China stepped up diplomatic efforts on Monday (Nov 2) to resolve the Middle East crisis, urging a return to the negotiating table in back-to-back meetings with Iranian, Omani and Saudi officials in Beijing.",
    body: "\"All parties should ... in a spirit of reason and pragmatism, focus on a political solution rather than once again resorting to armed conflict,\" said Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi.\n\nBEIJING: China stepped up diplomatic efforts on Monday (Nov 2) to resolve the Middle East crisis, urging a return to the negotiating table in back-to-back meetings with Iranian, Omani and Saudi officials in Beijing.\n\nThe meetings come days after US President Donald Trump issued a fresh war threat more than eight months into the conflict, which was triggered by US-Israeli strikes on Iran and has at times spilled over into neighbouring countries.\n\nBeijing supports Tehran in \"safeguarding its own sovereignty and security\", Foreign Minister Wang Yi told Ali Bagheri Kani, the deputy secretary of Iran's Supreme National Security Council, according to a Chinese statement.\n\nWang later called for dialogue to end the conflict \"while safeguarding regional energy security and the safety of shipping lanes\", as he met with Saudi National Security Adviser Musaad bin Mohammad al-Aiban.\n\nChina, a top economic partner of Iran, has long sought a detente in the months-long conflict that has disrupted global shipping.\n\nTrump said on Saturday that the United States had knocked out much of Iran's military but still has to \"take care of\" one or two targets, with Tehran warning of reprisals if attacked.\n\n\"All parties should ... in a spirit of reason and pragmatism, focus on a political solution rather than once again resorting to armed conflict,\" Wang told Bagheri Kani, according to the statement.\n\nChina's foreign ministry had not previously announced the visit by Bagheri Kani to Beijing.\n\nPresident Xi Jinping also met Oman's sultan for talks in Beijing on Monday - the first visit to China by the Gulf nation's head of state since diplomatic ties were established.\n\nXi told Haitham bin Tariq that China was ready to \"contribute to peace, stability and prosperity in the region\".\n\nThe Chinese leader said that the Middle East should not become a \"wrestling ground for major-country rivalry and geopolitical conflicts\", state news agency Xinhua reported.\n\nLater this month, Beijing will send its Middle East envoy to Iran, Oman and Saudi Arabia as a part of \"shuttle diplomacy visits ... aimed at promoting peace talks (and) easing tensions\", a Chinese foreign ministry spokesperson told a news briefing.",
